    expectedAnswer: "Offline State architecture keeps useful local state when the network is unavailable and reconciles it with the server later. The design must distinguish local persistence, pending operations, conflicts, and server truth.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the state-management concept.\n\nStep 1 — Track local data and pending operations separately.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface OfflineItem {\n  id: string;\n  value: string;\n}\n\ninterface OfflineState {\n  items: OfflineItem[];\n  pendingIds: string[];\n}\n\nfunction queueUpdate(\n  state: OfflineState,\n  item: OfflineItem,\n): OfflineState {\n  return {\n    items: state.items.some((value) => value.id === item.id)\n      ? state.items.map((value) =>\n          value.id === item.id ? item : value,\n        )\n      : [...state.items, item],\n    pendingIds: state.pendingIds.includes(item.id)\n      ? state.pendingIds\n      : [...state.pendingIds, item.id],\n  };\n}\n\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / persistence serialization:\n```ts\ninterface OfflineItem {\n  id: string;\n  value: string;\n}\n\ninterface OfflineState {\n  items: OfflineItem[];\n  pendingIds: string[];\n}\n\nfunction serializeOfflineState(\n  state: OfflineState,\n): string {\n  return JSON.stringify(state);\n}\n\nconst payload = serializeOfflineState({\n  items: [{ id: \"1\", value: \"draft\" }],\n  pendingIds: [\"1\"],\n});\n```\n\nExample:\nAn offline form saves drafts locally, queues mutations, and marks them pending until the server confirms them.\n\nComplexity:\nThe array implementation can be O(n) for update/duplicate checks.\n\nTrade-off:\nOffline behavior improves resilience but introduces conflict resolution, retry, and data-loss considerations.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain state ownership, source of truth, update flow, cache/rollback behavior, and the reason for choosing this approach.",
