    expectedAnswer: "Optimistic updates apply an expected UI change immediately and reconcile with the server afterward. They improve perceived latency but require rollback or compensation when the mutation fails.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the state-management concept.\n\nStep 1 — Keep the previous state so it can be restored.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface TodoState {\n  items: string[];\n}\n\ninterface OptimisticChange {\n  previous: TodoState;\n  next: TodoState;\n}\n\nfunction startOptimisticAdd(\n  state: TodoState,\n  item: string,\n): OptimisticChange {\n  const next = {\n    items: [...state.items, item],\n  };\n\n  return {\n    previous: state,\n    next,\n  };\n}\n\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / rollback helper:\n```ts\ninterface TodoState {\n  items: string[];\n}\n\ninterface OptimisticChange {\n  previous: TodoState;\n  next: TodoState;\n}\n\nfunction startOptimisticAdd(\n  state: TodoState,\n  item: string,\n): OptimisticChange {\n  return {\n    previous: state,\n    next: { items: [...state.items, item] },\n  };\n}\n\nfunction rollback(change: OptimisticChange): TodoState {\n  return change.previous;\n}\n\nconst change = startOptimisticAdd(\n  { items: [\"A\"] },\n  \"B\",\n);\n\nconst restored = rollback(change);\n```\n\nExample:\nA like button increments immediately; if the server rejects the request, the previous count is restored.\n\nComplexity:\nThe sample update is O(n) because it copies the array.\n\nTrade-off:\nOptimistic UI feels fast but requires reliable failure handling and clearly defined server truth.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain state ownership, source of truth, update flow, cache/rollback behavior, and the reason for choosing this approach.",
